    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Kittanning sits on the east bank of the Allegheny River, founded on the site of an 18th‑century Lenape village at the western end of the Kittanning Path.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        4,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kittanning%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The borough’s name derives from the Lenape word “Kithanink,” meaning “on the main river,” a reference to the Allegheny and Ohio together.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kittanning%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the French and Indian War, the 1756 Battle of Kittanning saw the village destroyed by John Armstrong Sr., a blow heard 44 miles away in Pittsburgh.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kittanning%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
